menu "Video Intercom"

    config SMART_INTERCOM
        bool "Build the smart_intercom component (two-way video intercom)"
        default n
        help
            Enables the smart_intercom component sources.

    config SMART_INTERCOM_AUTO_TEST
        bool "Build the on-device self-loopback test harness (auto_test/)"
        depends on SMART_INTERCOM
        default n

    config SMART_INTERCOM_DL_ZEROCOPY
        bool "Downlink video zero-copy (reassemble directly into decode slots)"
        depends on SMART_INTERCOM
        default n

    choice
        prompt "Downlink video target resolution"
        depends on SMART_INTERCOM
        default SMART_INTERCOM_DL_RES_720P
        help
            Selects the downlink (App -> device) H.264 target resolution the
            solution is built for. The actual decode geometry is still negotiated
            at runtime via doorbell.imageStream.setReceiveConfig; this choice only
            drives (a) the resolution advertised in doorbell.solution.getConfig's
            default template and (b) the coded access-unit slot capacity so the
            selected resolution's key frames fit without being dropped.

            720p keeps the stock library behavior and memory footprint. 1080p
            raises the coded-AU cap for larger key frames and roughly doubles the
            H.264 decoder recon pool (PSRAM MEM_SLAB_HEAP_UNCODED); review the
            downlink slot count and PSRAM budget before shipping 1080p, especially
            with the uplink camera and PIP running concurrently.

        config SMART_INTERCOM_DL_RES_720P
            bool "720p (1280x720)"

        config SMART_INTERCOM_DL_RES_1080P
            bool "1080p (1920x1080)"
    endchoice

    config SMART_INTERCOM_DL_SLOT_COUNT
        int "Downlink decode ring slot count"
        depends on SMART_INTERCOM
        range 3 8
        default 4
        help
            Number of slots in the downlink H.264 decode ring. The ring absorbs
            the rate mismatch between network frame reception (producer) and
            hardware decode (consumer), most notably the ~54ms IDR decode stall
            during which several P frames keep arriving. Too small drops P frames
            in that window and breaks the reference chain; too large adds
            end-to-end latency and consumes more MEM_SLAB_HEAP_CODED (each slot
            is about W*H*3/2 bytes; at 1280x720 that is ~1.38MB per slot, so
            3 slots is a practical minimum). Empirically 4~5 is a good balance
            at sub-720p; use 3 for 720p downlink. Upper bound is 8 slots.

    config SMART_INTERCOM_DL_REQUEST_IDR
        bool "Downlink request-IDR-on-loss feedback (doorbell.notify.requestKeyFrame)"
        depends on SMART_INTERCOM
        default y
        help
            When the downlink H.264 decoder hits a broken reference (decode
            failure from a lost frame), the device sends the JSON-RPC
            notification doorbell.notify.requestKeyFrame to the App so it can
            force an immediate IDR and shorten recovery latency (WebRTC FIR/PLI
            style). The notification is rate-limited (>=300ms between sends) to
            avoid an IDR-request storm.

    config SMART_INTERCOM_DL_WAIT_IDR
        bool "Downlink drop-until-IDR error resilience"
        depends on SMART_INTERCOM
        default y
        help
            The downlink H.264 stream is IPPP with P frames chained to the
            previous frame (max_num_ref_frames=1), so a single lost frame breaks
            the reference chain and every following P decodes to garbage / a
            hardware error until the next IDR. When enabled, the decode task
            enters a "wait for IDR" state after any decode failure and cheaply
            discards non-IDR access units (recycling their slot) until an IDR
            resynchronizes the decoder, avoiding the broken-reference decode and
            error-dump storm. Disable to always feed every AU to the decoder.

endmenu
